---
title: How to Get Your First Developer Job With No Experience in 2026
publishedAt: 2026-11-11
type: job-seekers
---
**Quick answer:** To get your first developer job with no professional experience, build two or three real projects that solve actual problems and deploy them, contribute to open source or freelance to get experience you can list, and write a one-page resume that puts projects first. Apply to roles labelled junior, associate, graduate, intern or apprentice, and widen your search beyond big tech to startups, agencies and non-tech companies that hire developers. Pair applications with direct outreach and referrals, and prepare for coding basics, a project walkthrough and behavioral questions.

Entry-level hiring is tougher than it was a few years ago. More people are learning to code, AI tools have raised the bar for what a junior developer is expected to do, and many companies prefer to hire experienced engineers. But companies still hire juniors every week. The ones who get hired usually aren't the ones with the most certificates. They're the ones with the most convincing proof they can build things.

## Is it still possible to get a developer job with no experience?

Yes. "No experience" usually means no full-time developer job yet, not no evidence of skill. Your goal is to replace the missing job history with other proof: projects, open source, freelance work, internships and a clear story.

## Step 1: Pick one direction

Junior candidates who apply for "any developer role" look unfocused. Pick a lane for the next three to six months:

{% table %}
- Track
- Core skills
- Typical first roles
---
- Frontend
- HTML, CSS, JavaScript, TypeScript, React
- Frontend developer, UI engineer
---
- Backend
- One language (Python, Java, Go or Node.js), SQL, REST APIs
- Backend developer, API developer
---
- Full-stack
- React or Next.js, Node.js or Python, a database
- Full-stack developer, software engineer
---
- Mobile
- Kotlin, Swift, React Native or Flutter
- Mobile developer
---
- Data
- SQL, Python, pandas, a BI tool
- Data analyst, junior data engineer
{% /table %}

You can switch later. Depth in one area beats a shallow tour of six.

## Step 2: Build projects that look like real work

Tutorial clones (a to-do app, a weather app) don't stand out anymore. Build things with real users, real data or real complexity.

**Good project ideas:**

- A tool that solves a problem for a local business, club or family member.
- An app that uses a public API in a useful way, with authentication, a database and error handling.
- A browser extension that saves you time.
- A dashboard built on real public data, with a clear question it answers.
- A rebuild of a small part of a product you use, with your own improvements.

**What makes a project convincing:**

- It's deployed and works when someone clicks the link.
- The README explains the problem, the stack, how to run it and what you'd do next.
- The code has tests, sensible commits and no secrets in the repository.
- You can explain every decision in an interview.

Two or three solid projects are better than ten half-finished ones. Our [portfolio guide](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/the-definitive-guide-to-crafting-a-standout-portfolio) has more detail.

## Step 3: Get experience you can put on a resume

Some ways to get "experience" before your first job:

- **Open source**: start with documentation fixes and "good first issue" labels, then work up to small features.
- **Freelance or volunteer work**: build a website for a non-profit or small business.
- **Internships and apprenticeships**: many are open to career changers, not only students.
- **Hackathons**: they show teamwork and shipping under pressure.
- **Teaching**: writing tutorials or mentoring beginners shows communication skill.

List these under "Experience" or "Projects" with the same structure as a job: what you built, the stack and the result.

## Step 4: Write a resume that leads with proof

For a first developer job, your resume should be one page:

1. **Header**: name, email, phone, city, GitHub, portfolio, LinkedIn.
2. **Summary** (optional): one or two lines on the role you're targeting.
3. **Projects**: your strongest section, with links.
4. **Experience**: freelance, internships, open source, or relevant non-tech jobs.
5. **Skills**: grouped clearly (languages, frameworks, tools).
6. **Education**: degree, bootcamp or self-taught courses.

Write bullets that show results: "Built a booking app used by 40 members of my climbing club, with Stripe payments and email reminders." Our [resume bullet point guide](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-write-resume-bullet-points-that-get-interviews-30-examples-for-tech-roles) has examples.

Check that your resume parses correctly with the [AI Resume Checker](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/ai-resume-checker).

## Step 5: Search in the right places, with the right titles

Search for these titles, not just "software engineer":

- Junior developer, associate engineer, graduate engineer
- Software engineer I, SDE 1
- Developer intern, apprentice developer
- Support engineer, QA engineer or technical support (good routes into engineering)

Look beyond big tech:

- **Startups** often care more about what you can build than your degree. See [how to get a job at a funded startup](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-get-a-job-at-a-funded-startup-in-2026).
- **Agencies and consultancies** hire juniors to work on client projects.
- **Non-tech companies** (banks, retailers, hospitals, logistics) all have engineering teams.
- **Remote roles** widen your options but get more applicants. Our [remote developer jobs guide for India](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/remote-developer-jobs-in-india-how-to-find-apply-and-get-hired-2026-guide) covers how to stand out.

On GetHired, you can describe what you want in plain English, such as "junior React roles, remote or Pune, posted this week", and the [agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/agent) ranks the best matches against your resume.

## Step 6: Don't rely on applications alone

Cold applications have low response rates for juniors. Combine them with:

- **Referrals**: ask friends, classmates, bootcamp alumni and former colleagues.
- **Direct messages** to engineers and engineering managers at companies you like. Keep it short and specific: what you built and why their company.
- **Community**: local meetups, Discord groups and online communities for your stack.
- **Being findable**: on GetHired, a public profile lets companies posting jobs find you and message you directly.

## Step 7: Prepare for junior interviews

Junior interviews usually include:

- **A coding exercise**: arrays, strings, hash maps, simple recursion. Practise explaining your thinking out loud.
- **A project walkthrough**: be ready to explain your architecture, trade-offs and what you'd improve.
- **Fundamentals**: HTTP, how the web works, Git, basic SQL, testing.
- **Behavioral questions**: use the [STAR method](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/the-star-method-for-software-engineers-how-to-answer-behavioral-interview-questions).

Our [25 common software engineer interview questions](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/top-25-software-engineer-interview-questions-and-how-to-answer-them) is a good place to start, and an [AI mock interview](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/ai-interview) lets you rehearse for a specific job.

## How should you use AI tools as a junior developer?

Employers expect you to use AI coding tools, but they want to see that you understand the code. In interviews:

- Be able to explain any code in your projects line by line.
- Say how you used AI: "I used it to scaffold tests, then fixed the edge cases it missed."
- Show judgement: when you checked its output, and when you didn't trust it.

## A realistic 90-day plan

